<h2>The Appeal</h2>
<p>The appellant stood convicted by the trial court of wrongful confinement, kidnapping, an unnatural offence and criminal intimidation under the IPC, together with aggravated penetrative sexual assault under Section 6 of the POCSO Act, 2012. The appeal — pending since 2018 — was heard and allowed by Justice C. Sudha, who set aside the conviction and acquitted the appellant.</p>

<h2>The Four Fractures in the Prosecution Case</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Medical inconsistency.</strong> The examination, conducted six days after the alleged incident, recorded no history of bleeding, no bruising or injury, and normal findings — despite an allegation of a prolonged assault causing severe pain. The prosecution offered no explanation for the absence of expected findings.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Forensic abandonment.</strong> Rectal swabs were collected during the medical examination but were never sent to the Forensic Science Laboratory. No report existed, and no witness explained the lapse.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Contradictory timeline.</strong> The FIR narrative had the child disclosing the incident upon feeling pain on a specific date; the Section 164 CrPC statement made no reference to pain triggering the disclosure — a material contradiction on the genesis of the case.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Arrest discrepancies.</strong> The prosecution witnesses gave conflicting accounts of when and where the accused was apprehended.</div>
</div>

<h2>The Motive Backdrop</h2>
<p>Days before the FIR, the accused had intervened in a quarrel involving neighbours. The Court noted the testimony suggested "the possibility of FIR being lodged in the backdrop of the quarrel cannot be completely ruled out." The judgment did not brand the complaint false; it applied the classical standard — where a real possibility consistent with innocence survives the evidence, the benefit belongs to the accused.</p>

<h2>Reading This Judgment with Its Companion</h2>
<p>Interestingly, the same Bench on the same day dismissed another POCSO appeal, affirming a conviction where the child\'s testimony was consistent and corroborated by an eyewitness sibling. The two outcomes are not in tension: together they mark the boundary of POCSO adjudication. Credible, consistent testimony convicts, even without medical corroboration; testimony beset by contradiction, unexplained forensic gaps and a plausible alternative genesis does not. The variable is the quality of the evidence, not the seriousness of the charge.</p>

<h2>Lessons for Investigation and Defence</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
<li>For investigators: forensic material once collected must be processed and produced; an unexplained gap in the chain invites an adverse inference that no closing argument can repair.</li>
<li>For prosecutors: reconcile the disclosure narrative across the FIR, the Section 164 statement and the deposition before trial, not after the defence exposes the divergence.</li>
<li>For the defence: contradictions on the genesis of the complaint — how, when and why the disclosure happened — are often more productive than attacking the child\'s account of the incident itself.</li>
<li>For appellate practice: long-pending appeals can succeed on the record alone; this conviction dated to 2018 and was undone entirely on the trial transcript.</li>
</ul>
</div>
